          "question": "Where does guidance differ on non-antibiotic management of uncomplicated UTI?",
          "answer": "Not medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.\n\nThe search found one relevant document, but the part I received doesn't answer your question.\n\nThe document is \"Uncomplicated urinary tract infection: treatment, culture indications and recurrence,\" from infectious diseases and urology society guidance. The excerpt I received defines uncomplicated infection as acute cystitis in a non-pregnant adult with a normal urinary tract and normal immune function. It also says that with typical symptoms, treatment can start without a urine culture, and that a dipstick test adds little in that group. It does not describe non-antibiotic management.\n\nThe document is marked as having a section on disagreements between guidance bodies, but that section was not in the results, so I can't tell you where guidance differs.\n\nThe other results were about sore throat and antibiotics, osteoarthritis, influenza and blood pressure. They are not about UTI, so I have not used them.\n\nTo get an answer, you could ask a doctor or pharmacist, or look up the full UTI guidance document, including its disagreements section.",
